    "lawCode": "Amd §17, Soc Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Social Services Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8643a\n\nSPONSOR: LIU\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the social services law, in relation to requiring the\ncommissioner to prepare a report of the sheltered and unsheltered home-\nless population during a state disaster emergency\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill would require the Commissioner of the Department of Health,\nthe Commissioners of the Department of Homeless Services, and the\nCommissioner of the New-York City Department of Health and Mental\nHygiene, in consultation with the local Departments of Health, to report\non the number of sheltered and unsheltered homeless individuals and\nfamilies during a state disaster emergency, declared pursuant to execu-\ntive order 202 of 2020 in response to the novel coronavirus (COVID-19)\npandemic.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill amends section 17 of the Social Services Law by\nadding a new subdivision (d-1) that creates reporting requirements for\nthe local Departments of Health on homeless individuals during a state\ndisaster emergency, declared pursuant to executive order 202 of 2020 in\nresponse to the novel coronavirus (COVID-19) pandemic. This bill would\nrequire the reporting of the number of infected individuals, number of\nhospitalizations, number of deaths, information regarding the individual\nincluding; age, race, family composition and shelter status including\nthe type of shelter utilized by the individual, if there are any known\nrisk factors, and any other information the commissioners deem neces-\nsary.\n\nThe Commissioners will aggregate data by county and post the reports to\neach respective agency's website.\n\nSection two of the bill establishes the effective date.\n\n \nDIFFERENCE BETWEEN ORIGINAL AND AMENDED VERSION (IF APPLICABLE):\n\nClarifies that state disaster emergency shall only refer to the state\ndisaster emergency, declared pursuant to executive order 202 of 2020 in\nresponse to the novel coronavirus (COVID-19) pandemic. Also, amends the\neffective date to reflect this change.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe impact of the COVID-19 virus on the homeless population in New York\nis still relatively unclear. We do know the virus will disproportionate-\nly impacted by this vulnerable population due to a lack of safe, private\nand clean-living spaces. Since the spread of the virus in New York, this\ngroup finds themselves at a particularly high risk of transmission,\nhospitalization, and death. In order to better protect the homeless in\nour state we must have as much information as possible. This legislation\naims to provide relevant information on the effects of the pandemic on\nthe homeless in New York with the hope of using this information to\nprovide adequate care and protections going forward.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2020: 5.8643 (Liu)\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately and shall apply retroactively\nbeginning March 7, 2020 and any time thereafter during the state disas-\nter emergency declared pursuant to executive order 202 of 2020 in\nresponse to the novel coronavirus (COVID-19) pandemic.\n",
